; Fish base as TWO sequential rabbit ears: triangle abd flattened at its
; incenter, then triangle cbd at its own — two independent single-vertex
; statements on opposite sides of the b-d line. Bare convention form (design
; 2026-07-17 §Segment inference): the leading pair (--l1)(--l2) fixes the
; stayer arc and the stayer filter picks --ray's surviving segment on its own,
; so the &-selectors drop.
;
; STATUS (2026-07-18): the SECOND ear does not close with first ear {toward .d}
; — every admissible stayer sector (and every emergent candidate and M/V
; pattern) self-intersects at the 12-face state. This is the pre-existing
; kernel block recorded in MEMORY ("Zwei-Ear-fish blockiert auf fehlender
; wahrer Realisierung (self-int)"): the mirror seating that carries the true
; chain is unreachable after the improper-anchor fallback was deleted
; (design 2026-07-17 §Solver-design pt.3). First ear {toward .b} DOES let the
; second ear close, but yields a different (14-face) state, not this intended
; fold. Resolving it is kernel work (Task 2 / a mirror-seating pass), not the
; eval wiring of this slice.

paper square

mark --diag = map .a onto .c
mark --ray = through .a .c

step left
mark --l1 = map --ab onto --diag
mark --l2 = map --da onto --diag
flatten (--l1) (--l2) (--ray) {toward .d}

step right
mark --l3 = map --cd onto --diag
mark --l4 = map --bc onto --diag
flatten (--l3) (--l4) (--ray) {toward .d}

; assert steps = 4
; assert faces = 12
